<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Yuryev-Polsky</h1><p class="st-meta">1212 CE – 1340 CE (approx.) · capital Yuryev-Polsky</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small appanage of the Vladimir-Suzdal lands, best known for the richly carved Cathedral of Saint George.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-dolgoruky">Yuri Dolgoruky</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuryev-polsky">Yuryev-Polsky</a> in 1152 in the open fields, the polye, between Vladimir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-the-big-nest">Vsevolod the Big Nest's</a> sons divided his lands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-vsevolodovich">Sviatoslav Vsevolodovich</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Yuryev</a> in 1212 and it became a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>It was a small agricultural principality whose rulers were closely tied to Vladimir. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-georges-cathedral-yuryev-polsky">Cathedral of Saint George</a> between 1230 and 1234, whose walls are covered with carved stone reliefs of saints, beasts and plants. The town was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1238.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav's</a> descendants held the principality as a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>. It came under the control of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> in the first half of the 14th century, and by around 1340 its separate princely rule had ended.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1212 CE – 1340 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.82%;width:2.55%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1212 CE – 1340 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yuryev-Polsky</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sviatoslav Vsevolodovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yuriev Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1276&amp;focus=principality-of-yuryev-polsky" aria-label="Principality of Yuryev-Polsky on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5539 257 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Yuryev-Polsky lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5859 513L5878 523M5859 513L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5878 523h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5859 513h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5859 513h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yuryev-Polsky. <a href="/atlas?year=1276&amp;focus=principality-of-yuryev-polsky">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
